{"task":"triaj concerns: bryce-m5.local","goals":"Repo-level concerns with no commit to hang on","status":"Waiting","concerns_owner":"bryce-m5.local","standalone_concerns":[{"id":"cn-b3814414bca7","text":"persite mode gives every site the same Caddy admin port, so the second site cannot start","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"complete","at":"2026-09-08T11:20:51.761Z","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","related":[],"resolved_at":"2026-09-08T11:37:49.827Z","resolved_by":"476ad4b44be9afecbeaabb1ca92c8236a379ab27","resolved_by_actor":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad"},{"id":"cn-c00f8ddf0002","text":"Per-site mode has no way to run a site as a root daemon, so port 443 is unreachable by design\n\npersite runs each site as a per-user LaunchAgent, and on macOS an unprivileged process cannot bind a specific address on a port below 1024. So a persite site can serve 443 to the whole network, or not serve 443 at all — it cannot serve 443 to loopback. The bare https://<site>.<tld> URL is therefore unavailable in the mode, with no hint from the tool that this is why. Restoring it for matsu.ldev needed a hand-written LaunchDaemon plist and a swap script kept outside the repo in ~/.config/ldev/matsu-443. If that is worth supporting, ldev new (or an ldev daemon <site> subcommand) could emit the daemon plist alongside the Caddyfile and file the one privileged step, instead of each site being rebuilt by hand.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T10:59:46.936Z","by":"claude:84a94ca3-be6f-45bb-bfed-90e7f8bab9ad","related":[]},{"id":"cn-01207326911e","text":"install.sh does not tidy up the mode it is replacing, so two topologies end up live at once\n\nObserved 2026-09-09 on this machine. The box was set up in persite mode; a later run of install.sh chose auto. That run rewrote ~/.config/ldev/config to MODE=auto and installed /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.ldev.caddy.plist, but left every per-site Caddy running and every per-site Caddyfile in place. Both topologies were then live: the auto daemon held port 80, a per-site daemon held 443, and https://ldev/ answered from neither — it returned 000 ten times out of ten, because the process on 443 has no site matching Host: ldev. Nothing warned, and nothing failed loudly; the install reported success. An install that changes MODE should either refuse while the other mode is running and say what is running, or take the other mode down as part of switching. Related: the config it writes then describes a machine that does not exist, which is the second concern filed alongside this one.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T15:29:22.402Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[]},{"id":"cn-225398076817","text":"ldev doctor trusts MODE from config rather than what is actually serving\n\nConsequence of the mode-switch gap. With MODE=auto written into ~/.config/ldev/config while the machine was really per-site, doctor took the auto branch and reported a failure for https://ldev/ while all three real sites were serving 200. The check was correct about the URL it probed and wrong about the machine. Two candidate fixes: cross-check MODE against what is on disk (per-site Caddyfiles under $SITES, /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.ldev.caddy.plist) and say so when they disagree, rather than silently believing the file; and/or have doctor report the mode it INFERRED next to the mode configured. Flipping MODE back to persite by hand made doctor green with no other change, which is the evidence that the machine was fine and only the config was wrong.","priority":"low","tags":[],"state":"open","at":"2026-09-09T15:29:22.564Z","by":"claude:eb23749b-b71d-44b3-ab30-5c8053a65845","related":[]}]}
